Z||000000||MrFeeArrow||01-29-2000||12:22 PM||||I know this has been dicussed many times before, but I can't find the topic in the search.<BR>Here we go.....  has anybody pulled the Iron Duke from the engine compartment, it looks like there is plenty of room?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||fierospeeder||01-30-2000||03:10 AM||||you have to try to do the search again, i know someone said it is possible to pull it up from the top.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||David||01-30-2000||11:54 AM||||I've pulled my 4 cyl from the top twice.  It is pretty straight foward except that you have to remove more accessories than what you would think.  Remove the trunk lid, the alternator and brackets, water pump, starter,  A/C compressor (don't remove the lines from the compressor).  I don't remember if I removed the exhaust manifold.  Leave the intake manifold and injector on, and don't remove the distributor.||||reg||1
Z||000003||tiacPaul||02-01-2000||07:24 PM||||I just disconnected everything from the engine and removed the four bolts holding it from the bottom.  I used a cart with wheels to support the engine and i could roll it around.  I then used my lift the lift the car up off the engine.  Everything was right there and easy to work on.  The mechanics at the shop said that from the bottom is the only eay to do it.  Note that the car weighs very little without the engine.  A board made a good bumper guard to lift the car by chain.  e-mail me any questions and let me know how it goes!||||reg||3
Z||000004||tiacPaul||02-01-2000||07:32 PM||||remember you need lots of room because the engine pulls off sideways from the transmision.  You need to get around the bell houseing. ||||reg||1
